 Republican lean and the possibility that Republicans consolidate behind Treg Taylor keep this short of Likely Democratic.", "watch": "If Republicans unify behind Taylor and ranked-choice transfers consolidate the 'rank the red' vote, the race could tighten quickly or flip.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Mike Dunleavy (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Alaska's governorship is open as Republican Mike Dunleavy terms out, and the general election features one Democrat, Jonathan Kreiss-Tomkins, against three Republicans led by Treg Taylor under ranked-choice voting. The state leans Republican by about six points, but this year's environment favors Democrats, and raters have shifted accordingly: Cook Political Report moved the race from Lean Republican to Toss-up, while Kalshi and PredictIt give the Democrat roughly a 75 percent chance.\n\nKreiss-Tomkins has raised more than twice as much as Taylor, though Taylor holds more cash on hand. The only recent public poll shows the Democrat narrowly ahead with many voters still undecided, and Republicans are divided over whether to urge supporters to 'rank the red.' The split Republican field and Alaska's underlying lean keep this genuinely competitive, but the weight of the evidence points modestly toward the Democrat.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.6825, "margin": 2.46}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7335, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7395, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7331, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.7068, "margin": 3.5}]}